      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Best Provinces for PNP in 2027: 2026 Allocations, Priorities and Which Province Fits Your Profile
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        There is no single "best" province, only the best fit for your occupation, location and status. We checked each province's own 2026 figures so you can compare like for like before 2027 allocations are announced.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>10 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">91,500</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">National PNP admissions target, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">92,500</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">2027 PNP target in the current levels plan</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">14,119</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Ontario's 2026 allocation, the largest</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        Every year, Immigration, Refugees and Citizenship Canada gives each province a fixed number of nominations. For 2026 those allocations rose sharply from the reduced 2025 starting levels, and several provinces received extra spaces in August. But a bigger allocation does not automatically mean a better chance for you. What matters is whether your occupation, job offer and current location match what a province is actually selecting. This guide compares the provinces with published 2026 figures, summarizes where each one is heading, and matches common profiles to the programs that suit them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Verified 2026 PNP Allocations</h2>

      <p>
        We only list figures published by the provinces themselves. Allocations exclude separate federal initiatives such as Alberta's additional physician and francophone spaces.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Province</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Initial 2026</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Latest 2026</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Usage reported</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Ontario (OINP)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">14,119</td><td class="px-4 py-3">14,119 (no increase posted)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not published on the OINP updates page</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Manitoba (MPNP)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,239</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8,000 (+1,761 in August)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">3,777 nominations, January to August</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Alberta (AAIP)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,403</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,603 (+200 on August 10)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,541 issued as of October 7</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">British Columbia (BC PNP)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,254</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,254 (+1,000 on August 18)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Plans to use the full allocation by December 31</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Saskatchewan (SINP)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,761</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,261 shown on SINP statistics page</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,023 nominations (76%)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Newfoundland and Labrador, PEI</td><td class="px-4 py-3" colspan="3">We could not find an official 2026 allocation published by these provinces. Figures circulating online are third-party estimates.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Saskatchewan's FAQ describes 4,761 as its initial allocation; its processing statistics page now shows a total of 5,261 without an accompanying announcement we could find. Treat the difference as an increase reported by the province but not yet explained publicly.</p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Key Takeaway</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">Mid-year top-ups are now a pattern. In 2026 Alberta, British Columbia and Manitoba all announced extra spaces in August. A province's January number is a floor, not a ceiling, which is one reason not to choose a province on allocation size alone.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Where Each Province Is Heading</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">British Columbia: Care, Build, Innovate</h3>
      <p>
        Since April 23, 2026, BC selects almost entirely through three objectives: <strong>Care</strong> (36 occupations in healthcare, education, childcare and veterinary care), <strong>Build</strong> (certified workers in 9 skilled trades) and <strong>Innovate</strong> (High Economic Impact invitations based on wage or score). Invitations in 2026 used wage thresholds from $52 to $70 per hour or scores from 131 to 138 for High Economic Impact. BC requires a job offer from an eligible B.C. employer, has no student streams, closed its Entry Level and Semi-Skilled stream, and since June 2026 excludes 12 occupations entirely. It expects at least 35% of nominations to go to workers outside Metro Vancouver. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2027-outlook-predictions" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027 BC PNP outlook</a> goes deeper.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Alberta: Broad Streams, Strong Usage</h3>
      <p>
        Alberta publishes stream-level allocations, which makes it unusually transparent. As of October 7, 2026: Alberta Opportunity Stream 3,562, Rural Renewal 1,057, Dedicated Health Care Pathways 518, Accelerated Tech Pathway 619, Express Entry priority sector draws 619, Tourism and Hospitality 156, Entrepreneur streams 60 and Law Enforcement Pathway 12. Alberta charges a $135 fee to submit a Worker Expression of Interest (since April 7, 2026) and allows EOI edits (since May 26, 2026). Additional federal spaces for practice-ready physicians (NOC 31100, 31101, 31102) and francophones with CLB/NCLC 5 in French sit outside the allocation; 50 physicians and 12 francophones had been nominated that way in 2026.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Saskatchewan: Sector Quotas</h3>
      <p>
        Saskatchewan reserves at least 50% of nominations for priority sectors (healthcare, agriculture, skilled trades, mining, manufacturing, energy and technology), including 750 spots for Saskatchewan post-secondary graduates working in those sectors. Accommodation and food services, trucking and retail are capped at a combined 25% and can only support current employees with six months or less left on their work permit, through short intake windows. Those windows have repeatedly filled on the day they opened in 2026. The province reported processing many worker categories in about two weeks in the third quarter of 2026.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Manitoba: The Biggest 2026 Increase</h3>
      <p>
        Manitoba's allocation rose from 6,239 to 8,000 in August. Its draws favour people already working in Manitoba: the October 8, 2026 draw issued 46 Letters of Advice to Apply to people employed in health occupations and 211 to those employed as secondary or elementary teachers, early childhood educators or teacher assistants. Skilled Worker selections in that draw were limited to candidates directly invited under a strategic recruitment initiative or covered by the federal temporary public policy for prospective PNP candidates. Manitoba issued 5,616 Skilled Worker Letters of Advice to Apply from January to August.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Ontario: A New Single Stream</h3>
      <p>
        Ontario replaced its eight streams with the <strong>Ontario Workforce Priority (OWP) stream</strong> through regulatory amendments in force on June 25, 2026, and opened OWP expressions of interest on August 4. OWP has a TEER 0–3 pathway (CLB 6, or CLB 5 for certain occupations; post-secondary education; and typically six consecutive months in the job offer position within the last 12 months, or two years in the occupation within five), a TEER 4–5 pathway (CLB 4; a Canadian secondary diploma or equivalent; nine months with the job offer employer within two years), and a pathway for self-employed physicians. Former Masters, PhD and Express Entry streams closed. On October 5, Ontario added 10 points for earning $80,000 or more in a single year within the past five years.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Nova Scotia: Healthcare and Trades First</h3>
      <p>
        Nova Scotia now runs four streams (Entrepreneur, Nova Scotia Graduate, Skilled Worker and Nova Scotia: Express Entry) and, in April 2026, set out priorities. Healthcare and skilled trades occupations at TEER 0–4 are the top priority and the only group considered for both international applicants and temporary residents already in Nova Scotia. Occupations in NOC broad categories 2, 4, 8 and 9 (TEER 0–4) may be considered for temporary residents already working in the province, other occupations only at TEER 0–2, and TEER 5 is not being prioritized. Expressions of interest have a 12-month validity period from May 1, 2026, and selections are not first-come, first-served.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Which Province Suits Your Profile?</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Your profile</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Stronger fits in 2026</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Watch out for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Nurse, physician or allied health worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC (Care draws, Health Authority stream); Alberta (Dedicated Health Care Pathways, extra physician spaces); Nova Scotia (top priority); Manitoba (health draws for local workers)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Licensing or registration is usually required before nomination</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Certified tradesperson</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC Build (9 trades, SkilledTradesBC certificate); Saskatchewan priority sector; Nova Scotia top priority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC requires certification matching the job, not just experience</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">High-earning professional or tech worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC High Economic Impact; Alberta Accelerated Tech Pathway (Express Entry); Ontario OWP</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC thresholds moved between $52 and $70/hour in 2026</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Hospitality, food service, retail or trucking worker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Ontario OWP TEER 4–5 pathway; Saskatchewan capped sectors; Alberta Tourism and Hospitality Stream</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Tiny or capped quotas; BC is not an option for TEER 4–5 or for 62010/62020 supervisors</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Early childhood educator or teacher</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC Care (ECEs; French-speaking K–12 teachers); Manitoba teacher and ECE draws</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Provincial certification is required in both</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Recent international graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Saskatchewan (750 spots for SK graduates in priority sectors); Nova Scotia Graduate stream</td><td class="px-4 py-3">BC and Ontario no longer run graduate-only streams</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">French speaker</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Alberta francophone spaces outside its allocation; BC priority for French-speaking teachers; federal francophone targets</td><td class="px-4 py-3">You need a valid TEF Canada or TCF Canada result</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        For head-to-head detail, see our comparisons of <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-alberta-aaip-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Alberta</a>, <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-ontario-oinp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Ontario</a>, <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-saskatchewan-sinp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Saskatchewan</a> and <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-manitoba-mpnp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC vs Manitoba</a>. Note that those were written earlier in 2026, before some of the changes above.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">A Worked Example</h2>

      <p>
        Take a registered nurse working in Calgary on a post-graduation work permit, with CLB 9 English. In Alberta, she is already working in the province, so the Dedicated Health Care Pathways are the natural route. In BC, she would need a B.C. job offer, ideally from a health authority (which can support a Health Authority stream application without a registration) or a private employer in a Care occupation. She would also need to register with the BC College of Nurses and Midwives. Moving provinces only makes sense if the B.C. job offer is genuine and she plans to stay. Use the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> to compare her registration score against recent Care: Health cutoffs, which ranged from 76 to 108 between May and October 2026.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to Expect for 2027 (Analysis)</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Allocations will arrive between December and February.</strong> In this cycle, Saskatchewan posted its initial 2026 allocation in December 2025 and Ontario announced on February 6, 2026. We expect a similar rhythm after the 2027–2029 Levels Plan is tabled, which is expected in early November 2026.</li>
        <li><strong>The national pot looks stable, if the current plan holds.</strong> The 2026–2028 plan set 91,500 PNP admissions for 2026 and 92,500 for 2027, within a total of 380,000 permanent residents each year. Any change will come in the new plan.</li>
        <li><strong>In-province workers will keep the edge.</strong> Manitoba's selections, Saskatchewan's capped-sector rules, Nova Scotia's priorities and BC's job-offer requirement all favour people already working in the province. We expect that preference to continue as the federal government pursues its goal of bringing temporary residents below 5% of the population.</li>
        <li><strong>Healthcare and trades remain safe bets.</strong> Every province profiled here prioritizes healthcare, and most prioritize trades. Hospitality, retail and general administrative roles face the most restrictions and we see no sign that will ease in 2027.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Choosing a Province</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Choosing by allocation size.</strong> Ontario's 14,119 spaces are shared by the most candidates; a smaller province that targets your occupation can be the better bet.</li>
        <li><strong>Moving without a job.</strong> Most worker streams need a job offer or work history in the province. A move with no offer rarely helps.</li>
        <li><strong>Treating a nomination as portable.</strong> A nomination is for living in that province. BC's guide, for example, requires that you intend to live, work and economically establish in B.C.</li>
        <li><strong>Relying on 2026 rules in 2027.</strong> Ontario and Nova Scotia restructured mid-year. Check each program's official page before you act.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which province has the largest PNP allocation in 2026?</h3>
          <p>Ontario, with 14,119 nominations announced on February 6, 2026. Manitoba is second after an August increase took it to 8,000, followed by Alberta at 6,603, British Columbia at 6,254 and Saskatchewan, whose processing statistics page shows 5,261.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Have the 2027 PNP allocations been announced?</h3>
          <p>No. As of October 14, 2026, no province has a 2027 allocation. The federal 2026–2028 Immigration Levels Plan sets a national PNP target of 92,500 admissions for 2027, but the 2027–2029 plan expected in November 2026 may revise it. Among the provinces we checked, 2026 allocations were posted between December 2025 and February 2026.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which province is best for healthcare workers?</h3>
          <p>Most provinces prioritize healthcare. British Columbia runs targeted Care draws and a Health Authority stream, Alberta has Dedicated Health Care Pathways plus extra federal spaces for practice-ready physicians, Nova Scotia lists healthcare as a top priority open to international applicants, and Manitoba has held draws for health workers already employed in the province.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Which provinces still nominate hospitality, retail or trucking workers?</h3>
          <p>Options are limited. Saskatchewan caps these sectors at 25% of its allocation and only accepts current employees with six months or less left on their work permit during short intake windows. Alberta has a small Tourism and Hospitality Stream, and Ontario's new Ontario Workforce Priority stream has a TEER 4-5 pathway. BC's Skilled Worker stream is limited to TEER 0-3 and excludes retail and food service supervisors.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">Can I apply to more than one provincial nominee program?</h3>
          <p>Each province runs its own system, and nothing in BC's guide bars you from registering elsewhere, but every PNP requires a genuine intention to live in that province and you can only use one nomination for permanent residence. BC also limits you to one active BC PNP registration or application at a time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

